To convert a `std::string` to a `std::vector<char>` (or a `std::vector` of the string's character type), you can use the range constructor of `std::vector`.

Here are some common ways to do it:

### Example

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::string s = "hello";

    // Method 1: Using vector range constructor
    std::vector<char> v1(s.begin(), s.end());

    // Method 2: Using assign()
    std::vector<char> v2;
    v2.assign(s.begin(), s.end());

    // Method 3: Initializing from std::string data and size
    std::vector<char> v3(s.data(), s.data() + s.size());

    // Print out vector content
    for(char c : v1) std::cout << c;  // prints "hello"
    std::cout << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

### Notes

- The vector will hold a copy of the characters in the string.
- You can also use `std::vector<unsigned char>` or other types as needed, but be aware of type conversions.
- If you want to include the null terminator `\0` character (if your string is null-terminated and you want that in the vector), you might do:

```cpp
std::vector<char> v(s.c_str(), s.c_str() + s.size() + 1);
```

This copies the string data including the terminating null character.

---

**Summary:**

```cpp
std::string s = "example";
std::vector<char> v(s.begin(), s.end());  // converts string to vector of chars
```

This is the simplest and most idiomatic way.

